WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.060
SPEAKER 1: My friend Kelsey
Hightower once said, containers

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.550
are the price of admission
to using Kubernetes.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.440
So to get started
with Kubernetes,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.132
we'll need to dive deeper into
what containers really are.

00:00:11.132 --> 00:00:12.840
With a whole universe
of things to learn,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.120
it's important to start
with the essentials.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.620
Let's go over the
concepts that make

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.980
Kubernetes usable, scalable,
and just downright awesome.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.450
Are you ready?

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.173
So Kubernetes is a
container orchestrator.

00:00:24.173 --> 00:00:25.590
Last time you said
that containers

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.400
were packaged up services.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.627
What does that actually mean?

00:00:28.627 --> 00:00:30.210
SPEAKER 2: Yeah,
containers were built

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.500
to deploy smaller services
with isolation and consistency.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.660
But they're not
actually a new concept.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.790
Containers are based on
some underlying concepts

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.230
from the Linux kernel--

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.450
Namespaces and Cgroups.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.220
SPEAKER 1: Right,
Namespaces make sure

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.200
that individual
processes can't see

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.060
the details of other processes.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.490
And Cgroups control how
much resources a process

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.680
can use, like CPU memory.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.330
SPEAKER 2: These
two concepts are all

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.820
about giving a
process isolation,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.460
protecting it from interfering
with other processes,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.807
as well as protecting
itself from others.

00:01:02.807 --> 00:01:05.099
SPEAKER 1: Virtual machines
can give us a similar level

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:08.550
of isolation, but only by
packing the whole operating

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.760
system with it.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.510
For plenty of applications,
the OS isn't necessary.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.210
And it certainly takes
up a lot of resources.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.340
Containers, on the
other hand, only focus

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.560
on the application
and its dependencies,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.210
making them more lightweight.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.070
Now apps don't
need to worry about

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.760
if some developer had hard-coded
Python 2.7 into the path

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:27.390
variable.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.560
SPEAKER 2: That's an
oddly specific example.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.840
But yes.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.810
There's actually a few
different container run times.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.840
But one of the most
popular is Docker.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.380
They built an API that helped
app developers build containers

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.290
and a platform to run them the
same way regardless of which

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.502
machine they run on.

00:01:44.502 --> 00:01:45.210
SPEAKER 1: Right.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.970
It's all about letting the app
developer define what they want

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.850
the runtime to look like instead
of spending time messing around

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.150
with the kernel.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.810
This is all done
in a Docker file.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.100
Why don't we zoom in on one?

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.240
SPEAKER 2: Good idea.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.000
If you were working
on a simple Flask app,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.410
your Docker file
might look like this.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.850
The first line is the base
image the app should use.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.650
You can choose from all
sorts of base images.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.770
So you can choose one
that has exactly what you

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:14.610
need with less overhead,
making your container smaller.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.860
SPEAKER 1: Next, these
lines so the container

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.397
what commands to run
when being initialized.

00:02:19.397 --> 00:02:21.480
Your Flask app is definitely
going to need Python.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.330
So this installs
Python, Pip, and Flask.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:25.830
SPEAKER 2: Now the
Copy command will

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.050
grab files from your
local environment

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.860
and copy them into
the container.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:36.360
So your local app.py file
gets copied to /opt/app.py.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.890
And the container now
has your app files.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.070
SPEAKER 1: And finally,
the Entry Point command

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.050
lets your container
run as an executable,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.420
actually starting up
the Flask application.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.000
With just a few lines,
you've created a container.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.040
SPEAKER 2: Awesome, but--

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:50.970
SPEAKER 1: But?

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.428
SPEAKER 2: But you
would still have

00:02:52.428 --> 00:02:54.000
to manually deploy
the container,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.660
manage it, and handle situations
like if the container goes down

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.660
or if you want to roll out
an upgrade to your app code.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.970
SPEAKER 1: And now we've
come back to Kubernetes.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.370
Kubernetes takes care of
managing your containers,

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.465
no matter if you
have one or 1,000.

00:03:07.465 --> 00:03:09.090
Just like how your
Docker file explains

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.770
what you want your
container to do,

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.690
you can configure
Kubernetes for what you

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.520
want your whole cluster to do.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.440
You might hear this
called the desired state.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.440
SPEAKER 2: And that's
probably the coolest thing

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.210
about Kubernetes.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.490
You tell it what the desired
state for the cluster is,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.120
and it automates everything from
pods, nodes, to load balancers,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:27.780
and more.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.100
SPEAKER 1: Whoa.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:29.730
Hold on.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.530
That's a lot of new stuff.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.570
Let's start with the essentials.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.700
We were just talking
about containers.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.230
So what's a pod?

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.970
SPEAKER 2: A pod is
like a collection

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.850
of multiple containers, sort
of a pseudo-application.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:44.610
It could just be
one app, or it could

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.310
be multiple different ones
that need to work together.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.177
For example, let's go
back to your Flask app.

00:03:50.177 --> 00:03:50.760
SPEAKER 1: OK.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.130
So I know how to
build a container.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:55.440
How do we turn this into a pod?

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.600
SPEAKER 2: Well, if
that's your desired state,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.220
we can tell Kubernetes
to make it happen.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.350
Here's a YAML file that gives
the desired state for a pod.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.200
This metadata section
gives a way for Kubernetes

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.810
to group pods and other
resources together.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.040
And we'll dive deeper
into that later.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.683
For now, the key part
is this Spec section.

00:04:14.683 --> 00:04:16.350
SPEAKER 1: Here's
where you name the pod

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.300
and tell it which image to use.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.560
Since this is a simple pod.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.567
Your container is the
only one running here.

00:04:21.567 --> 00:04:23.400
There's also a port
section so the container

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.180
can be accessed through the
web on the default ACT port 80.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.910
SPEAKER 2: Once this config is
put into a Kubernetes cluster,

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.260
Kubernetes knows
what you want to do

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.040
and is in charge of ensuring
that your actual state matches

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.600
your desired state.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.690
SPEAKER 1: That's a
great start, but where is

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:41.517
the pod actually going to run?

00:04:41.517 --> 00:04:43.350
SPEAKER 2: For that,
we'll need to talk more

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.630
about nodes and deployments.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.580
Let's cover that in
the next episode.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:48.540
SPEAKER 1: Sounds good.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.120
So today, we covered
more about containers

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.950
and why they became so popular.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.250
SPEAKER 2: Then we started
to talk about how Kubernetes

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:56.670
works with containers.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.515
But there's still
plenty more to cover.

00:04:59.515 --> 00:05:00.140
SPEAKER 1: Wow.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.570
That was a lot of great
info on containers.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:06.680
But now, we need to
figure out how they run.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.090
Stay tuned, everybody.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:09.863
If you want to get
hands on, check out

00:05:09.863 --> 00:05:11.030
the link in the description.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.730
And if you enjoyed the
episode, subscribe for more.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:17.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

